var PHQ=[{"s": "ESR", "p": "3", "q": "What are the factors affecting ESR?", "a": ["1) Plasma proteins: ESR increases when fibrinogen and globulins increase. They are positively charged, so they reduce the negative charge of the RBCs, which lowers RBC repulsion and helps rouleaux formation.", "ESR also increases when albumin decreases.", "2) RBC count."], "src": "f", "lo": 0}, {"s": "ESR", "p": "2-3", "q": "Name the physiological and pathological variations in ESR.", "a": ["Increased, physiological: in females during menstruation and pregnancy; in increased temperature; after muscular exercise.", "Increased, pathological: inflammation and tissue destruction (acute and chronic infections e.g. tonsillitis, appendicitis, tuberculosis; severe trauma e.g. fractures; myocardial infarction; degenerative and neoplastic disease; rheumatic arthritis); all anemias except spherocytosis, sickle cell anemia and pernicious anemia; all malignant diseases; nephrosis (albumin drops, fibrinogen and globulins rise); trauma, surgery and any large-scale tissue injury.", "Decreased: polycythemia; anemias of spherocytosis, pernicious anemia and sickle cell anemia; afibrinogenemia."], "src": "f", "lo": 0}, {"s": "ESR", "p": "3", "q": "What is the clinical significance of ESR?", "a": ["ESR changes in a great variety of conditions, so it is not specific (not diagnostic) for any particular disease and gives no indication of the nature of the underlying disorder.", "Repeated ESR estimations help in: 1) prognosis, 2) follow up of the activity of the disease, 3) its response to treatment."], "src": "f", "lo": 0}, {"s": "ESR", "p": "1", "q": "Compare the two ESR methods (Wintrobe and Westergren).", "a": ["Wintrobe: oxalate is used as anticoagulant (citrate would dilute the sample too much).", "Westergren: citrate is used as anticoagulant. It is more sensitive than Wintrobe because its tube is long enough and has a larger diameter.", "Disadvantage of Westergren: citrate dilutes the red cells, which increases ESR, but fibrinogen and globulin are also diluted, which decreases ESR. Both neutralize each other."], "src": "f", "lo": 0}, {"s": "ESR", "p": "1-2", "q": "How is ESR measured by the Westergren method?", "a": ["Principle: with anticoagulant, the heavier red cells settle gradually in a narrow vertical tube. The rate in mm at the end of one hour is the ESR.", "Procedure: add 2 ml of venous blood to 0.5 ml Na-citrate (3.8%); mix but do not shake; suck the mixture into the Westergren tube up to mark 0; place it upright in the stand (22-27 C); after one hour read in millimeters the column of clear plasma above the sedimented RBCs.", "Tube: 300 mm long, diameter 2.5 mm, graduated 0-200."], "src": "f", "lo": 0}, {"s": "ESR", "p": "2", "q": "What are the ESR values in the results table?", "a": ["Male: first hour 5, second hour 8. Female: first hour 10, second hour 16.", "Note: the file writes the unit as \"ml\" in the table, but its principle says the ESR is in mm."], "src": "f", "lo": 1}, {"s": "Hemoglobin", "p": "6", "q": "What are the normal levels and ranges of Hb at different ages?", "a": ["Male: 16 +/- 2 g/100 ml blood.", "Female: 14 +/- 2 g/100 ml blood.", "Newborn: 18 to 22 gm%."], "src": "f", "lo": 0}, {"s": "Hemoglobin", "p": "6", "q": "Compared to females, why are the hemoglobin levels higher in males?", "a": ["The file gives only the values (male 16 +/- 2, female 14 +/- 2 g/100 ml) and does not explain the reason.", "Temporary answer, not from the file, to be verified: testosterone in males stimulates erythropoietin and red cell production, so Hb is higher. In females, menstrual blood loss and the lack of this androgen effect keep it lower."], "src": "t", "lo": 0}, {"s": "Hemoglobin", "p": "6", "q": "Why is the hemoglobin level high in the newborns?", "a": ["The file gives only the newborn value (18 to 22 gm%) and does not explain the reason.", "Temporary answer, not from the file, to be verified: in the womb the fetus has relative hypoxia, which raises erythropoietin and red cell production, and fetal hemoglobin (HbF) carries oxygen with higher affinity. So the newborn has a high Hb at birth."], "src": "t", "lo": 0}, {"s": "Hemoglobin", "p": "6", "q": "What are the common causes of increased and decreased Hb readings?", "a": ["Increased: polycythemia.", "Decreased: anemia."], "src": "f", "lo": 0}, {"s": "Hemoglobin", "p": "4", "q": "What is the principle of Hb estimation (Sahli acid hematin method)?", "a": ["The Hb in a measured amount of blood is converted by dilute HCl into acid hematin, which is golden brown when diluted.", "The color intensity depends on Hb concentration.", "After dilution with water, the color is matched against golden brown glass rods."], "src": "f", "lo": 0}, {"s": "Hemoglobin", "p": "5", "q": "Describe the procedure of the Sahli method.", "a": ["1) Fill the graduated tube with 0.1 N HCl to the 20% mark (2 gm) or a little more, until the pipette tip is submerged.", "2) Sterilize the thumb tip with alcohol and let it dry.", "3) Prick with a sterile lancet, wipe away the first 2 drops, take 20 microliters of blood with the micropipette. Do not squeeze the finger.", "4) Wipe the blood on the pipette tip.", "5) Transfer the blood into the acid by immersing the tip at the bottom; expel gently and rinse the pipette 3-4 times.", "6) Mix with the stirrer until uniformly dark brown (acid hematin) and place beside the standard brown tubes. Dark brown at the bottom means poor mixing.", "7) Wait about 5 minutes.", "8) Add distilled water drop by drop until the color matches the standard tubes.", "9) Take the average of 3 readings: slightly darker than standard, exactly matching, slightly lighter."], "src": "f", "lo": 0}, {"s": "Hemoglobin", "p": "5-6", "q": "What is the difference between absolute and relative Hb scales?", "a": ["Absolute scale: the Hb tube is graduated directly in gm% (0-24).", "Relative scale: the tube is calibrated in percentage of normal (100% = 17 gm%), graded 0-160%. It is an old method."], "src": "f", "lo": 0}, {"s": "Blood indices", "p": "7", "q": "What is MCV and how is it calculated?", "a": ["Mean corpuscular volume: the average volume of a single RBC.", "MCV = (H.V / No. of RBCs in millions) x 10 = 90 cubic micron (normal 74-95)."], "src": "f", "lo": 0}, {"s": "Blood indices", "p": "7", "q": "What is MCH and how is it calculated?", "a": ["Mean corpuscular hemoglobin: the average amount of Hb in one RBC.", "MCH = (Hb / No. of RBCs in millions) x 10 = 30 picograms (normal 27-32 pg).", "In macrocytic anemia MCH may be as high as 39 pg but MCHC stays within normal."], "src": "f", "lo": 0}, {"s": "Blood indices", "p": "7", "q": "What is MCHC and how is it calculated?", "a": ["Mean corpuscular hemoglobin concentration: the average amount of Hb in 100 ml RBCs. It shows the actual Hb content in red cells only (not the whole blood).", "MCHC = (Hb / H.V) x 100 = 34 gm/dl (normal 30-36). Another way: MCHC = MCH / MCV.", "Normal range: normochromic. Below normal: hypochromic. It cannot be hyperchromic because the maximum Hb capacity of RBCs is 36%."], "src": "f", "lo": 0}, {"s": "Blood groups", "p": "8", "q": "Explain the ABO system (agglutinogens and agglutinins).", "a": ["People are divided into 4 groups by the presence or absence of 2 agglutinogens (A and B) on the red cells. Plasma has specific agglutinins (Anti-A and Anti-B).", "Group A has Anti-B in plasma. Group B has Anti-A. Group AB has no antibodies. Group O has both Anti-A and Anti-B.", "Rule 1: if an agglutinogen is present on the red cells, the matching agglutinin must be absent from the plasma. Rule 2: if an agglutinogen is absent, the matching agglutinin must be present."], "src": "f", "lo": 0}, {"s": "Blood groups", "p": "9", "q": "How do you determine the ABO and Rh groups?", "a": ["ABO: mark one end of the slide A and the other B; sterilize the finger, lance it, put one drop of blood on each end; add anti-A serum to side A and anti-B serum to side B; mix by rocking the slide 1-2 minutes; look for agglutination (looks like red pepper grains).", "Rh: mix 2 drops of blood with one drop of anti-D serum on a dry clean slide; rock gently 2-3 minutes; check under the microscope if in doubt. The Rh reaction is weaker and less easy to detect than A and B."], "src": "f", "lo": 0}, {"s": "Blood groups", "p": "9", "q": "What is the Rh system?", "a": ["The Rh antigen is present only on the surface of RBCs. Rh positive means the D antigen is present (85% of the population). Rh negative means the D antigen is absent (15%). It is detected by strong anti-D serum."], "src": "f", "lo": 0}, {"s": "Blood groups", "p": "8, 10", "q": "Importance of the ABO system (the lines are blank on p.10; this is what the file says about it).", "a": ["The file says ABO and Rh are the blood group systems of clinical importance (p.8).", "Clinical significance (p.8 objectives): it is essential to match donor and recipient blood groups before a transfusion, because mismatched transfusion has harmful effects. It also gives the basis of the terms universal donor and universal recipient (Landsteiner's law).", "Basis (p.8): group A has anti-B, group B has anti-A, group AB has no antibodies, group O has both anti-A and anti-B. If an agglutinogen is present in the red cells, the matching agglutinin is absent from the plasma; if it is absent, the agglutinin is present."], "src": "f", "lo": 0}, {"s": "Blood groups", "p": "10", "q": "Importance and dangers of the Rh antigens (p.10). What does the file say about Rh antibodies?", "a": ["Significance (p.10): Rh incompatibility results in a hemolytic transfusion reaction and hemolytic disease of the newborn.", "Unlike the ABO system, there are no naturally occurring antibodies against Rh antigens in Rh negative people.", "Immune antibodies develop against Rh antigen after exposure following transfusion or pregnancy, and can be detected by the Coombs test (antiglobulin test)."], "src": "f", "lo": 0}, {"s": "Hemostasis", "p": "11", "q": "Define hemostasis and thrombosis.", "a": ["Hemostasis (Greek: haema = blood, stasis = halt): the process of stoppage of bleeding after blood vessels are damaged.", "Thrombosis: clotting of blood within an unbroken blood vessel, usually by a roughened endothelium due to atherosclerosis, infection or injury."], "src": "f", "lo": 0}, {"s": "Hemostasis", "p": "11", "q": "List the tests for hemostasis.", "a": ["Bleeding time, clot lysis time, coagulation time, capillary fragility test (Hess test), platelet count, Ivy bleeding time, PT and PTT, clot retraction time.", "Bleeding time and coagulation time are simple tests used as a routine before every minor and major surgery (e.g. tooth extraction, biopsy) and before and during anticoagulant therapy."], "src": "f", "lo": 0}, {"s": "Hemostasis", "p": "11-12", "q": "What is bleeding time and how is it measured (Duke method)?", "a": ["Bleeding time: the time from the skin injury to the stoppage of bleeding. It depends on local vasoconstriction and platelet plug formation (a test of platelet function).", "Duke method: sterilize the earlobe; scrub with alcohol to increase blood flow; small injury (3-4 mm deep) with a sterile pin; remove blood with filter paper at intervals without squeezing or pressing; record the time from injury to stoppage.", "Fingertip is not preferred (thick in some people). If bleeding goes on more than 10-12 minutes, stop, press with sterile gauze and inform the teacher."], "src": "f", "lo": 0}, {"s": "Hemostasis", "p": "12", "q": "What is the normal bleeding time and what does it depend on?", "a": ["Normal bleeding time = 1-5 minutes.", "It depends on: depth of the wound, rate of blood flow to the skin, the platelet count, and the condition of the blood vessel wall."], "src": "f", "lo": 0}, {"s": "Hemostasis", "p": "12", "q": "What are the causes of prolonged bleeding time?", "a": ["I. Platelet disorders: a) thrombocytopenia (number below 50,000/cmm, quantitative): primary (unknown cause) or secondary (aplasia of bone marrow due to X ray, radioactive substances, neoplastic deposits, drug sensitivity). b) thrombasthenia (defect in function, qualitative): primary, or secondary (severe uremia, von Willebrand disease, drugs such as aspirin).", "II. Vascular disorders: vitamin C deficiency."], "src": "f", "lo": 0}, {"s": "Hemostasis", "p": "12-13", "q": "What is coagulation time and what are its methods and normal values?", "a": ["Coagulation time: the time from start of bleeding to formation of fibrin threads. It depends on blood coagulation factors.", "A. Capillary blood: 1) Wright capillary glass tube method (more accurate): normal 3-6 min. 2) Drop method (pin drawn through a drop every 30 seconds): normal 2-4 min.", "B. Venous blood (Lee and White tube method, tube in water bath at 37 C tilted every 30 seconds): normal 5-10 min."], "src": "f", "lo": 0}, {"s": "Hemostasis", "p": "13", "q": "What are the causes of prolonged coagulation time?", "a": ["1) Hemophilia (A = factor VIII, B = IX, C = XI). 2) Vitamin K deficiency. 3) Liver disease (factors II, VII, IX and X) and obstructive jaundice. 4) Use of anticoagulants."], "src": "f", "lo": 0}, {"s": "Hemostasis", "p": "14-15", "q": "What is a thrombus and what are emboli? What are the causes and complications?", "a": ["Thrombus: an abnormal clot that develops in a blood vessel. Emboli: freely flowing clots.", "Causes of thromboembolic conditions: 1) a roughened endothelial surface (arteriosclerosis, infection or trauma); 2) blood often clots when it flows very slowly.", "Complications: obstruction of arterial supply gives ischemia and necrosis of the organ; obstruction of venous drainage gives edema. Emboli from large arteries or the left heart plug arteries or arterioles in brain, kidneys or elsewhere. Emboli from the venous system or the right heart cause pulmonary arterial embolism."], "src": "f", "lo": 0}, {"s": "Hemostasis", "p": "15", "q": "What is the prophylaxis and treatment of thromboembolism?", "a": ["Prophylaxis: avoid prolonged recumbence; use anticoagulants in susceptible patients.", "Treatment: TPA to dissolve the clot."], "src": "f", "lo": 0}, {"s": "Hemostasis", "p": "15", "q": "Compare heparin and dicumarol.", "a": ["Heparin (injected): origin mast cells and basophils; facilitates antithrombin III (against IXa, Xa, XIa, XIIa); acts in vivo and in vitro; rapid onset; short duration; by injection; antidote protamine sulfate 1%.", "Dicumarol (oral): plant origin; inhibits activation of vitamin K so inhibits formation of prothrombin and factors VII, IX, X; acts only in vivo; delayed onset; long duration; given orally; antidote vitamin K.", "Blood transfusion is the antidote for both."], "src": "f", "lo": 0}];
